            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book provides a compelling structural definition of a 'Name' by tracking it to the Ark of the Covenant, which Scripture explicitly aligns with the Name (2 Sam 6:2, 2 Sam 7 / 1 Kgs 8). By inventorying the Ark (Heb 9:4) as the Word (manna), Law (tables), and Priesthood (rod), the book moves the common interpretation of 'authority and identity' from an abstract concept to a concrete, scripturally defined covenant structure. This framework elegantly explains why miracle-workers claiming Jesus' name are rejected for 'lawlessness' (Matt 7), how taking the name 'in vain' means claiming the label while emptying it of its substance (Ex 20:7), and why the women in Isaiah 4:1 seek the name while refusing the head's provision and covering. The counter-interpretation, while historically common, treats Name as a general metonymy for character or authority and cannot explain the specific architectural substitution of the Ark for the Name or the mechanics of claiming a name 'in vain' as thoroughly.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The Name is the covenant identity of a house, consisting of its Head's Word, covenant law, and fruitful priesthood.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Substituting any one creates a counterfeit Name.\",\n    \"Rejecting, replacing, or removing any one takes the Name in vain.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The assertion that the Lord's Prayer maps directly to the three specific elements of the Ark.\",\n    \"The conclusion that the 'daily bread' petition refers primarily to the manna/Word, which risks downplaying its literal application.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The Name symbolizes the person themselves—their character, presence, and delegated authority. The connection between the Name and the Ark in 2 Samuel and 1 Kings reflects Deuteronomistic 'Name theology' (God causing His presence to dwell in the sanctuary) rather than the physical contents of the Ark defining the ontological components of a 'Name' universally.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A Name is the personal identity, presence, and delegated authority of the individual it represents.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that the concept of a Name is essentially defined by or strictly consists of the specific triad of Word, covenant law, and priesthood, treating those instead as historical features of the Sinai covenant rather than the universal definition of a Name.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"The common biblical consensus holds that 'name' stands for character, identity, and authority.\",\n    \"Passages like Exodus 3:14-15 ('I AM') define God's name by His eternal nature, independent of the Ark or its contents.\",\n    \"The substitution of 'House for the Name' for 'House for the Ark' in 2 Samuel/1 Kings demonstrates that the Temple is where God's presence dwells, not that the Ark's contents are the literal definition of 'Name'.\",\n    \"Acting 'in the name of' Jesus (Col 3:17) means acting by His authority, not necessarily invoking the specific triad of Word, Law, and Priesthood.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Explains the explicit textual substitution between a house for the Ark and a house for the Name in the Davidic narrative (2 Sam 7, 1 Kgs 8).\",\n    \"Gives concrete scriptural mechanics to the abstract concept of 'authority' by defining it through the Ark's contents (Word, Law, Priesthood).\",\n    \"Explains Matthew 7:22-23 by showing why claiming the Name while practicing 'lawlessness' fails the test of the Name's substance.\",\n    \"Provides a precise definition for taking the Name 'in vain' (emptying it of its constituent parts) rather than limiting it to false oaths or profane speech.\",\n    \"Explains the marriage imagery of Isaiah 4:1 (claiming the name without submitting to the house/head's provision).\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Accounts for everyday and non-theological uses of names in narrative and genealogies without forcing a covenantal structure onto them.\",\n    \"Aligns smoothly with God's self-revelation in Exodus 3, where His Name is grounded in His being ('I AM') prior to the giving of the Law or the construction of the Ark.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"2 Samuel 6:2\",\n    \"1 Kings 8:20-21\",\n    \"Hebrews 9:4\",\n    \"Exodus 20:7\",\n    \"Isaiah 4:1\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
